  In conclusion, Botswana’s population composition is characterized by its youthful demographic
  profile, with a significant proportion of children, adolescents, and young adults shaping the nation’s
  present and future. While this presents opportunities for economic growth and development,
  it also poses challenges in terms of meeting the diverse needs of different age groups and
  ensuring equitable access to opportunities and services for all segments of the population. By
  understanding and addressing the dynamics of its population composition, Botswana can better
  navigate the opportunities and challenges of demographic change and work towards building a
  prosperous and inclusive society for all its citizens.
  Table 5.3 shows the population composition across different age groups and sex composition.
  The results indicate a higher percentage of population in younger age groups compared to older
  age groups. Age group 0-4 years (10.9 percent) has the highest share of population followed by
  subsequent age groups respectively with age group of 100 years and above having the lowest
  share of population.
  The results further suggest that the proportion of males is higher than that of females in age
  groups less than 20 years; while that of females is higher than that of males in all other age groups
  except for age group 45-49 years. At national level, the results indicate a higher proportion of
  females (51.2 percent) than males (48.8 percent) as depicted in Table 5.3 above.
