  The distribution of sex and locality illustrated in Table 2 above shows that the majority of youth were
  living in urban villages (405,504) rather than rural areas (234,814). This is quite notable, as it means that
  the youth population is unevenly distributed. Such uneven distribution often leads to strains on social
  amenities and labor resources, hence potentially contributing to high rates of youth unemployment and
  inequality compared to the general population.
