  Population & Housing Census 2022:
  4.
  Key Demographic and Socio-Economic Indicators
  2.0 Population size and distribution
  The population of Botswana is currently estimated at 2,359,609 which was an increase of 334,705 persons
  from 2,024,904 estimated in 2011. The population grew at 1.4 percent per annum for the period with the
  oldest enumerated persons aged at 106 years. Although the population of Botswana is still youthful, there
  is an increase in the mean age estimated at 28.1 years from 26 years in 2011. The population is still skewed
  towards females with a sex ratio of 95 males to 100 females. About 44.5 percent of enumerated households
  were headed by females with a mean age of 47.6 years. It is also important to note that female headed
  households tend to be larger than that of their male counterparts.
  3.0 Population geographic spread
  Botswana is a sparsely populated country with a density of 4.1 inhabitants for every square kilometre. Urban
  areas generally have higher population density compared to rural areas and settlements. For example,
  the cities of Gaborone and Francistown had a population density of 1444.4 and 1296.8 persons per square
  kilometre respectively.
  4.0 Fertility levels
  Against the backdrop of a population growth rate that continues to decline, the fertility is also declining. On
  average a woman is estimated to give birth to 2.9 children throughout her reproductive life. It is also noted
  that the median age of first child bearing continues to increase probably due to deliberate efforts to delay
  first pregnancy in order to develop one’s life career or for education pursuit.
  5.0 Mortality levels
  The results show that mortality is declining. Declining mortality levels may in part reflect improvement in the
  health of the population or results of deliberate investment in the health of the people. The expectation
  of life has increased modestly from 68 years in 2011 to current 70.94. On another note, the proportion of
  children who die before they reach their first birthday (infant mortality rate) is estimated at 27.92 per 1000
  live births.
  6.0 Access to social amenities
  It is estimated that 75.8 percent of Botswana population has access to electricity, 76.3 percent have access
  to a mobile phone and a further 85.4 percent have access to clean water.
  7.0 National registration
  A national registration is compulsory for all persons who have attained the age of sixteen. Citizens are issued
  a national card for identification and accessing services such as schooling or health care. An estimated
  86.7 percent of the population aged above fifteen years reportedly had national registration cards.
  It is estimated that 97.8 percent of all deaths are registered with the authority. On the other hand, an
  estimated 89.5 percent of children’s births were reportedly registered with the authorities.
  Dr. Burton Mguni
  Statistician General
  December 2023
  Statistics Botswana
